; NextDAAD SP18 Task 1 fixture - font/pointer switching.
;
; Compiled by tests\build-tests.ps1 on every run. This is a STIMULUS
; fixture: what matters is the condact bytes DRC actually emits, not
; the game being playable, so there is no staging switch and no leg
; folder for it here - tests\build-tests.ps1 asserts the compiled
; tests\out\fontsw.ddb bytes directly (DRC rewrites some condacts
; silently, which is exactly why the assertions exist rather than
; trusting the source text).
;
; GFX n 16 is this interpreter's font-switching sub-command: install
; font 2 (a file font, GFX 2 16), revert to font 0 (the base - the
; embedded table, then FONT.CHR over it if one is staged), then ask for
; font 3, which nothing stages, so the load must fail silently and font
; 0 must still be showing.
;
; THE POINTER SECTION IS LIVE, NOT STATIC. The first cut of this fixture
; only ever called MOUSE n 5 (POINTERMS, pick a shape) and waited on
; ANYKEY - it never called MOUSE n 3 (GETMS), which is the ONLY condact
; that polls the Kempston mouse and repositions the hardware sprite
; (src\overlay0.asm h_mouse.read / mouse_sprite_pos). On real hardware
; that showed the shapes changing colour at the reset position and never
; moving. Each shape below now runs a live tracking loop instead of a
; single wait, so the owner can move the mouse under it and watch the
; shape change while it tracks:
;
;   $poll
;   > _ _   MOUSE 100 3   ; GETMS: poll + reposition the sprite
;           INKEY         ; non-blocking; fails when no key is down,
;           SKIP $next    ; which aborts this entry right here
;   > _ _   SKIP $poll    ; INKEY's failure lands here (the next entry -
;                         ; src\engine.asm eng_exec's "condition failed"
;                         ; path is eng_next_entry, one header on) and
;                         ; jumps back
;
; INKEY (condact 111, h_inkey) is a condition, not an action, and it is
; non-blocking - it succeeds the instant a key is down and fails
; otherwise, and a failed condition aborts only the CURRENT entry, not
; the table. That is why the loop needs two entries: the poll entry's
; SKIP $next only runs when INKEY succeeded, reached by falling through
; (a failed INKEY never gets there); the entry immediately after it is
; what a failed INKEY lands on, and its only job is SKIP $poll, back to
; the top. SKIP (condact 116, h_skip) moves the CURRENT table's entry
; pointer by a signed count of entries and does not mark the turn done
; (cprops row $C1, the "action but not for ISDONE" kind it shares only
; with REDO), so it cannot terminate the table by itself - entries fall
; through to the next one automatically (DRC emits a $FF terminator on
; every entry; eng_exec's .endentry case is just eng_next_entry) until
; something explicit (DONE, NOTDONE, END, table end) stops it. Flags
; 100-103 are free for author use (flags 64-254 are), so MOUSE 100 3
; writing them is safe and unasserted by name below - only the condact
; bytes are checked.
;
; THE HOTSPOT IS SET ONCE, BEFORE ANY SHAPE, AND MUST SURVIVE ALL THREE.
; MOUSE 8 6 / MOUSE 8 7 (DELTAXMS/DELTAYMS) offset the pointer's hotspot
; 8 pixels right and 8 down from the bitmap's own (0,0), and that is a
; documented guarantee of the feature: a POINTERMS shape change (MOUSE
; n 5, h_mouse.pointer) must not reset it back to (0,0) - h_mouse.pointer
; never touches mouseHotX/mouseHotY, only h_mouse.reset (MOUSE 0 0) does.
; Nothing tested that guarantee before this fixture; each shape's message
; below calls it out so the owner can confirm the offset holds across
; every MOUSE n 5 call in the sequence, not just the first.
;
; ANYKEY, NOT "PAUSE 0". This fixture is compiled with a plain
; "DRF.exe zx next" and no -v3, so its header version is 2 and PAUSE 0
; would mean 256 frames, not a key (this is also why the tracking loop
; above uses INKEY/SKIP rather than a V3 GETKEY). ANYKEY (opcode 24,
; ZERO parameters) is the V2 wait-for-key condact this interpreter has
; always implemented, and it is still used for the three font waits.
;
; DEBOUNCE. A tap is still down for several frames, and the tracking
; loop is unthrottled (INKEY is non-blocking, so it polls as fast as
; the CPU allows), so the very next loop would otherwise see the same
; keypress and exit instantly. PAUSE 25 sits right after every loop
; exit - authored 25 because 25 x DRC's ZX NEXT PAUSE scaling (0.6,
; tests\sfxdi.dsf's own header has the full derivation) is exactly 15
; frames (0.3 s at 50 Hz), with no rounding to argue about.
;
; SYSMESS 16 carries a trailing #n below, the same override sfxdi.dsf
; uses and for the same reason: h_anykey prints it before blocking, and
; without the newline the next MESSAGE would print on the same line as
; "Press any key.". The tracking loop never calls ANYKEY, so its own
; MESSAGE n calls carry their own "press a key" wording instead.
;
; No picture, no audio, no save file, no typed input - PRO 0 runs the
; whole sequence at boot and ends with END, so no parser loop is needed.

/CTL
_
/VOC
NORTH  2  noun
SOUTH  3  noun
LOOK   4  verb
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I can't do that."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key.#n"
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0 "FONT 2 - this line should look different#n"
/1 "FONT 0 - back to the base font#n"
/2 "FONT 3 is absent - this must still read as font 0#n"
/3 "Pointer shape 2 - blue. Offset 8 pixels right and 8 pixels down from the cursor position, on purpose - watch that it holds through every shape change below. Move the mouse, then press a key to continue.#n"
/4 "Pointer shape 1 - red. Same 8/8 offset - confirm it is still there after this shape change. Move the mouse, then press a key to continue.#n"
/5 "Pointer shape 0 - the base pointer. No POINTER.SPR is staged here, so this must be the interpreter's own built-in arrow - black outline, white fill, tip at the top left. Same 8/8 offset - confirm it is still there after this last shape change. Move the mouse, then press a key to continue.#n"
/OTX
/0 "a brass lamp"
/LTX
/0 ""
/1 "The font lab."
/CON
/0
NORTH 1
/1
SOUTH 0
/OBJ
/0    1   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   _ _
/PRO 0
> _ _   GFX 2 16      ; install font 2
        MESSAGE 0
        ANYKEY
        GFX 0 16      ; revert to the base font
        MESSAGE 1
        ANYKEY
        GFX 3 16      ; absent font: must be a silent no-op
        MESSAGE 2
        ANYKEY
        MOUSE 0 0     ; reset/centre (also re-zeroes any earlier hotspot)
        MOUSE 0 1     ; SHOWMS - show the pointer
        MOUSE 8 6     ; DELTAXMS - hotspot 8px right of the bitmap's (0,0)
        MOUSE 8 7     ; DELTAYMS - hotspot 8px down; must survive every
                      ; MOUSE n 5 shape change below (SP18 B23 guarantee)
        MOUSE 2 5     ; pointer shape 2
        MESSAGE 3

$poll2
> _ _   MOUSE 100 3   ; GETMS: poll the mouse, reposition the sprite
        INKEY         ; key down? if not, this entry aborts here
        SKIP $next2   ; key down: out of the loop
> _ _   SKIP $poll2   ; no key: go round again

$next2
> _ _   PAUSE 25      ; debounce before the next loop's INKEY can see
                      ; the same keypress that just ended this one
        MOUSE 1 5     ; pointer shape 1
        MESSAGE 4

$poll1
> _ _   MOUSE 100 3
        INKEY
        SKIP $next1
> _ _   SKIP $poll1

$next1
> _ _   PAUSE 25
        MOUSE 0 5     ; back to the base pointer
        MESSAGE 5

$poll0
> _ _   MOUSE 100 3
        INKEY
        SKIP $next0
> _ _   SKIP $poll0

$next0
> _ _   PAUSE 25
        END
/END
